 race close, so a modest Democratic margin with wide uncertainty fits the evidence.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling in October, or a shift in the national House environment, could move this back to a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 64 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Mike Lawler (R) is running."]}, "overview": "New York's 17th district is one of the House's most competitive seats, where first-term incumbent Mike Lawler faces Democrat Cait Conley in a district that leans Democratic by about a point. Expert raters are divided: four call it a Tossup while five, including the Economist and Silver Bulletin, put it as a Lean D flip. Prediction markets on Kalshi, Polymarket and PredictIt all price Conley around 65-67%.\n\nThe two available polls are sparse and sponsor-leaning, averaging to roughly even, and the newest is from July. Lawler holds a major financial advantage with $4.8 million on hand, and has drawn cross-party endorsements, while Conley has strong outside spending support and high-profile Democratic surrogates like Pete Buttigieg campaigning for her. The race sits as a narrow Democratic lean with substantial uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7399, "margin": 4.04}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7185, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6936, "margin": 3.0}]}